this is a production of cornell  university  this is frank rossi for the cornell turf  grass video log  and we're talking about annual bluegrass  seed heads in the lawn  now in your lawn areas you'll often see  seed heads in the spring  particularly on annual bluegrass now in  most lawns annual bluegrass is going to  function as a true winter annual  which means it germinates from seed in  the fall over winters as a clump of  tillers  and then the following spring you'll see  it produce flowers  which is the seed heads that you see now  those seed heads  is the shift of the plant from the  reproductive phase  from the vegetative phase to the  reproductive phase and then  after that reproductive phase that  tiller will die  you will see a natural thinning in that  lawn on the other side of that annual  bluegrass  as a winter annual wants to go through  the summer as a seed  not as a plant the only reason annual  bluegrass would ever survive  in most of our lawn situations is  because we're sustaining it with lots of  water and fertilizer  through the summer months otherwise that  plant would easily just  die off after the flowering so be  mindful of that that you will cease a  natural decline in the lawn  after the annual bluegrass seed heads  are produced  and if you want to get them off of  course a rotary mower  will be able to take the seed heads off  long term if you want to solve that  problem there are very few chemical  options  but there are some that are available  for the most part the best thing to do  is to just let it dry out  and then as it thins out you can reseed  those patches come the following fall  this is frank rossi for the cornell turf  grass video  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu
 

